; ==============================================================================
; BUILD-TIME OBSERVATION: what a program observes belongs to the process it RUNS in
; ==============================================================================
; `lin build` EVALUATES the program, so every call the build performs is one the artifact
; does not have to -- and that is only honest while the call is a function of its
; OPERANDS.  A call that reaches outside the net is the program's own OBSERVATION, and
; making one at build time answers a question the program was going to ask later, in a
; different process: measured before the rule, `lin build` of test/runtime_ffi.lin read
; `N` from the BUILD host's environment and froze that answer into the artifact -- built
; with N=2 the artifact printed 3 for every N, built without N it printed 1 where the
; interpreter prints 7.
;
; The rule is ONE GATE in the core (src/io.c `lin_build_gate`), asked by every dispatch
; path before it performs a call, and it answers by CAPABILITY: a provider DECLARES, where
; it registers, whether calling it observes the world (`lin_scalar_ops_add(..., pure)`),
; a driver declares the same for its own rows (std/drivers/readback.c `ffi_row_pure`), and
; a call NOBODY declared is refused -- the sound default for a foreign symbol.  A build
; that reaches such a call does not make it: it REPORTS what it stopped at, leaves the
; call's redex in the net, and the artifact makes it at run time, in its own process.
;
; This suite is that case end to end, and aot_equiv.py pins all of it: the build must not
; RUN the command (its own run of this suite must produce no OBSERVED line), it must SAY
; what it stopped at, and the ARTIFACT must decide from the environment of the process the
; artifact is in -- which is the only environment the program was ever about.  The value
; here is the command's EXIT STATUS (256 for a shell exiting 1, as ffi_systems.lin's `; expect 0`
; already pins for a success), so the two answers are different numbers rather than different text.
(load "std/std.lin")

; Pure work first, so that what the build KEEPS is visible in the artifact: nothing here
; depends on the world, so a build may (and does) evaluate all of it.
(define! sumto (num -> num) (\n (ifl (is_zero n) (\_ 0) (\_ (add n (sumto (pred n)))))))

; ... and then the observation: a shell asking whether this process has LIN_BUILD_OBSERVE.
; A build that ran it would bake the BUILD host's answer into the artifact; a build that
; declines leaves the question to the run, and aot_equiv builds this suite with the
; variable ABSENT and runs it with the variable SET, exactly as it does for runtime_ffi.
(add (sumto 30) (ccall1 "system" "test -n \"$LIN_BUILD_OBSERVE\" >/dev/null 2>&1"))
; expect => 721
